isky because if someone needs B5 for a win, they might claim it. But since I have a pair, discarding one B5 reduces my chance to pung B5, but I still have one B5 left. However, if I discard B5, I break the pair, and then I would need to draw either a third J3 or a chow with T6. That's less efficient.  \n\nDiscarding J3: I have two J3, discarding one leaves one J3, so I lose the chance for Dragon Pung. But I could still pung B5. But as said, B5 pung gives 8 points (All Pungs + two Pung of Terminals/Honors). So it's acceptable. However, discarding J3 might be risky because J3 is a dragon, and someone might need it for a dragon pung or win. Looking at history, across player discarded J3 once, so maybe they don't need it. Next player and previous player haven't shown interest in dragons.  \n\nBut overall, the safest discard is probably T6, because it is a simple tile with no special value, and it doesn't break any of my pairs. Keeping both pairs maximizes my chances to draw a pung (either B5 or J3). Also, T6 is not likely to complete a chow for others? Across player has T2-T3-T4 chow, so T6 is not adjacent to that. Previous player has B2 pung, so not related. Next player has no melds. So T6 seems safe.  \n\nTherefore, I will discard T6.  \n\nReason: Discarding T6 keeps both pairs (B5 and J3), giving me two chances to draw a pung for win. Completing J3 pung yields 10 points, completing B5 pung yields 8 points, both sufficient. T6 is relatively safe and does not benefit opponents' evident patterns.  \n\nAnswer: Play T6"}
